Suppose you have an application server:

Hostname: appserver
IP:       192.168.1.50

You can connect using IP:

ssh user@192.168.1.50

but this doesn't work:

ssh user@appserver

That means the problem may be hostname → IP resolution.

1. First understand what is happening

When you type:

ssh user@appserver

Linux needs to know:

"What IP address belongs to appserver?"

For example:

appserver  →  192.168.1.50

This is called name resolution.

If Linux cannot find the IP, hostname connection won't work.

2. First test — getent hosts

Run:

getent hosts appserver

a) Case 1 — You get an IP

For example:

192.168.1.50    appserver

Good!

This means:

appserver
   ↓
192.168.1.50

Linux can resolve the hostname.

So DNS/name resolution is probably not the problem.

b) Case 2 — Nothing comes back

Example:

$ getent hosts appserver

$

This means Linux could not find an IP for appserver.

Now we investigate DNS/name resolution.

3. Check /etc/resolv.conf

Run:

cat /etc/resolv.conf

You may see something like:

nameserver 192.168.1.1

The important part is:

nameserver

It tells Linux:

"Which DNS server should I ask when I need to convert a hostname into an IP?"

Think of it like:

Your Linux machine
       |
       | "What is the IP of appserver?"
       ↓
DNS Server
       |
       ↓
192.168.1.50

If the DNS server is wrong/unreachable, hostname resolution can fail.

4. Check resolvectl status

Run:

resolvectl status

This gives more information about DNS configuration.

You may see something like:

DNS Servers: 192.168.1.1

or:

DNS Servers: 8.8.8.8

You can check:

Which DNS server is being used
Which network interface is using it

5. Test with dig

Now run:

dig appserver

You are basically asking DNS:

"DNS, do you know the IP address of appserver?"

If DNS knows it, you may get:

ANSWER SECTION:

appserver.     60    IN    A    192.168.1.50

That means:

appserver → 192.168.1.50

DNS is working. ✅

If dig says something like:
status: NXDOMAIN

It means:

DNS does not know this hostname.

So the problem is likely DNS/name registration.

6. Then check network connectivity

Suppose:

getent hosts appserver

returns:

192.168.1.50    appserver

Great.

Now test whether you can reach the server:

ping -c 4 appserver

If you get:

64 bytes from 192.168.1.50

then the machine is reachable.

7. But ping working doesn't always mean application works

This is important.

Suppose:

appserver
   ↓
192.168.1.50
   ↓
Network works

But the application is listening on port 8080.

Test:

nc -vz appserver 8080

If successful:

Connection to appserver 8080 port [tcp/*] succeeded!

Then:

Hostname resolution ✅
Network connectivity ✅
Port connectivity ✅

If it fails, then investigate the application/service/firewall.

=> Complete troubleshooting flow

Think of it like this:

You type:
ssh user@appserver
        |
        ↓
Can Linux find the IP?
        |
        ↓
getent hosts appserver
        |
        +---- NO ----→ DNS problem
        |               |
        |               ├─ cat /etc/resolv.conf
        |               |
        |               ├─ resolvectl status
        |               |
        |               └─ dig appserver
        |
        YES
        |
        ↓
Can you reach the server?
        |
        ↓
ping appserver
        |
        YES
        |
        ↓
Is the application port working?
        |
        ↓
nc -vz appserver PORT
        |
        YES
        |
        ↓
Check application/login
